# rclone-onedrive-tray configuration
# ---------------------------------
# Shell-style KEY="value" assignments. Read by both `onedrive-sync` (bash, via
# `.`) and `onedrive-tray` (python, via a tiny parser). Variable expansion such
# as $HOME works in both.
#
# After changing REMOTE, LOCAL, FILTERS_FILE or BISYNC_ARGS you must rebuild the
# sync baseline once:   onedrive-sync --resync

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ what to sync
# An rclone remote, optionally with a sub-path. Create/verify it with:
#     rclone config          # set up a remote named e.g. "onedrive"
#     rclone lsd onedrive:   # list what rclone sees
REMOTE="onedrive:"

# The local directory to keep in sync. Created if missing.
LOCAL="$HOME/OneDrive"

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ scheduling
# Base name of the systemd user units (installed as <UNIT_NAME>.service/.timer).
UNIT_NAME="onedrive-sync"

# Minutes between automatic syncs (measured from the end of the previous run).
INTERVAL_MIN="5"

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ safety
# Abort a run if it would delete more than this many files. A guard against a
# wiped-out folder propagating to the cloud.
#
# The number is a count of files, and it is translated into what rclone actually
# reads, a percentage of the pair: 100 of a 750-file folder becomes
# --max-delete 13%. Two consequences are worth knowing.
#
# 0 refuses every deletion. A number at least as large as the whole folder means
# no cap at all, and the log says so, because rclone's own default of 50% only
# applies when the flag is absent. Raise this if you legitimately delete large
# folders; docs/TROUBLESHOOTING.md covers the translation and how to recover from
# a trip.
MAX_DELETE="100"

# Abort a run when a marker file is missing on either side of the pair. This is
# rclone's own access check and a second, independent safety net beside
# MAX_DELETE: a network, authorisation or mount problem leaves one side looking
# empty, and without the check bisync reads that as "everything over there was
# deleted". With it on, bisync looks for the marker file in the same places on
# both sides before it changes anything.
#
# Off by default, so an existing run keeps the command line it had. Turning it on
# is a configuration change, and the first run after that fails until the marker
# files exist:
#
#     onedrive-check-access     # creates them, safe to run twice
#
# then set CHECK_ACCESS="1" and run one `onedrive-sync --resync`. The abort names
# the side it could not read, and nothing is copied or deleted when it trips.
# docs/TROUBLESHOOTING.md has the message and the recovery.
CHECK_ACCESS="0"

# Name of the marker file the check looks for. Empty means rclone's own default,
# RCLONE_TEST, which bin/onedrive-check-access creates.
#
# Upstream recommends a filename your tree already uses in many places, because
# the check compares the count and the directories on both sides: a name that is
# everywhere makes a partial mount or a half-readable tree abort the run, while
# one marker at the root only proves the root is readable. The tradeoff is that
# this script cannot create a file you already own, so its contents, and where it
# lives, become your problem: pick one that is already in the same places on both
# sides and leave it alone. For example:
#
#     CHECK_FILENAME=".sync-id"
CHECK_FILENAME=""

# Extra arguments handed to `rclone bisync`.
#   --resilient         retry after less-serious errors instead of demanding --resync
#   --recover           auto-recover from an interrupted run  (needs rclone >= 1.65)
#   --max-lock 2m       treat a lock older than 2m as stale    (needs rclone >= 1.65)
#   --conflict-loser num / --conflict-resolve none
#                       keep BOTH versions of a conflicting file (no data loss)
#   --stats 2s          emit progress blocks often enough for the tray to show
#                       a live percentage while a sync is running
# Do not put --force in here. It bypasses rclone's own delete cap, which would leave
# MAX_DELETE as the only guard left, and the log warns when it sees it.
BISYNC_ARGS="--resilient --recover --max-lock 2m --conflict-resolve none --conflict-loser num --stats 2s"

# rclone filter rules, one per line (rclone --filters-file syntax).
FILTERS_FILE="$HOME/.config/rclone-onedrive-tray/filters.txt"

# Names of top-level folders to leave off this machine, one per line, relative to
# REMOTE. This is what the tray's "Folders to sync" menu edits and what setup.sh
# writes when you deselect folders.
#
# A deselected folder is left alone on both sides: measured on rclone 1.75.1,
# excluding a folder touches neither the local copy nor the cloud, so nothing is
# lost and nothing is freed either. The tray offers to delete the local copy
# separately when you untick one. Toggling needs no --resync. Empty or missing
# means sync all of it.
EXCLUDE_FOLDERS_FILE="$HOME/.config/rclone-onedrive-tray/exclude-folders.txt"

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ tray
# Where the sync log is written. Also rotated by onedrive-sync at 5 MB.
LOG="$HOME/.cache/rclone-onedrive-tray/sync.log"

# Optional: an application the tray can launch from its menu, e.g. "obsidian".
# Leave empty to hide the menu entry.
OPEN_APP_CMD=""
OPEN_APP_NAME="the app"

# Tray language: "en" or "zh". Empty = follow the system locale (a zh_* locale
# selects Chinese, anything else falls back to English).
# Failure hints shown in the tray are localised too: onedrive-sync tags them in
# the log ([network], [maxdelete], ...) and the tray renders the matching text.
UI_LANG=""

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ realtime
# Watch the local folder and start a sync as soon as files change, instead of
# waiting for the timer. Requires the inotify-tools package.
# Changes made on another machine still rely on the timer, since rclone has no
# server-side push and nothing local can notice them.
WATCH="1"

# Seconds of quiet after the last change before a sync is triggered.
WATCH_DEBOUNCE="8"

# Seconds of quiet used to drain the events a sync raises by writing locally.
# Raise this if you ever see two syncs fire back to back.
WATCH_SETTLE="12"

# Optional regex of path fragments for inotifywait to ignore.
WATCH_EXCLUDE=""

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ advanced
# How many times to retry a failed sync before giving up for this round.
RETRIES="3"
# Seconds between retries.
RETRY_DELAY="60"
